        <li>
          <p>
            Is soil more compact (more dense) before or after logging? How can you tell? Which number(s) best support your answer?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            In which data set is variation in soil density greatest? How do you know?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Boxplots of residential fresh water use for all counties in Maryland, Louisiana, and Ohio are displayed below. Values are expressed in gallons per person per day. 
      </p>
      <image width="100%" source="statistics/3_6/Residential Water Boxplots.png">
      </image>
      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Suppose that you live in Louisiana, and your county ranks at the 1st quartile in water use. How much water, on average, does each person in your county use each day? 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            In which state does the median county use the least water, on a per capita basis?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            In which state is the spread in county water use the least, on a per capita basis?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            For these three states, is county per capita water use left-skewed or right-skewed?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The follow graphs compare the number of hurricanes in three regions: Atlantic, Eastern Pacific, and Central Pacific. The data is from the years 1991 to 2024. What characteristics of the distribution are apparent in the histogram, but not in the boxplot? What characteristics are apparent in the box plot, but not in the histogram?
      </p>
      <image width="100%" source="statistics/3_6/histogram_by_year.png">
      </image>
      <image width="100%" source="statistics/3_6/boxplot_by_year.png">
      </image>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Answer the following questions about standard deviation:
      </p>
      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Do outliers affect the standard deviation?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Suppose a data set has a point that is much lower than the rest. What type of effect (if any) would this have on the value of the standard deviation? 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Suppose a data set has a point that is much higher than the rest. What type of effect (if any) would this have on the value of the standard deviation?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Which of the following graphs has the smallest standard deviation?
          </p>
          <image width="50%" source="statistics/3_6/Histogram A.png">
          </image>
          <image width="50%" source="statistics/3_6/Histogram B.png">
          </image>
          <image width="50%" source="statistics/3_6/Histogram C.png">
          </image>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Arkshell clams (also known as blood clams) are harvested commercially off the coast of Virginia. A sample of two species of these clams (Noetia ponderosa and Anadara ovalis) had shell heights normally distributed with a mean of 20.3 mm and a standard deviation of 1.4 mm.<fn>U.S. Dept of Commerce, “Population Structure of the Arkshell Clam,” by Katherine McGraw, Sally Dennis, and Michael Castagna, NOAA technical report (1996). As cited in J. Susan Milton, Statistical Methods in the Biological and Health Sciences, 3rd ed. (Boston: McGraw Hill, 1999), 189.</fn>
      </p>
      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            For the Arkshell sample, find the seven numbers <m>\bar{x}-3s</m>, <m>\bar{x}-2s</m>, <m>\bar{x}-s</m>, <m>\bar{x}</m>, <m>\bar{x}+s</m>, <m>\bar{x}+2s</m>, and <m>\bar{x}+3s</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Write a compound inequality expressing the shell heights that would be considered unusual.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The U.S. Weather Service has tracked the number of reported tornadoes in Oklahoma since 1950.<fn>https://www.weather.gov/oun/tornadodata-ok-1950</fn> Download the following spreadsheet to view the annual and monthly breakdowns for the years 1950 to 2018. 
      </p>
      <p>
        <alert>Link:</alert> <url>https://1drv.ms/x/c/a1600d824c56be4e/IQDqJVdbShdCTK2DbbNnCmp0AfcIM0ealaB7Ce2XliGsoes?e=0m7yNM</url>
      </p>
      <ol marker="a.">
        <li>
          <p>
            Calculate the mean number of tornadoes per year and the standard deviation. Interpret the standard deviation in the context of the problem.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            In 1974, there were 45 tornadoes in Oklahoma. Is this value unusual? Why or why not? 
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            In 1999, there were 145 tornadoes in Oklahoma. Is this value unusual? Why or why not? 
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
